re-policy twist (pricing pressure/drug pricing legislation).\n  \u2022 Factor exposure: small-mid cap value with a growth tilt; AMRX is NOT a low-beta\n    defensive (\u03b2 = 1.255). It behaves like a cyclical/growth hybrid.\n  \u2022 Relationship to other likely holdings: AMRX has low correlation to large-cap tech;\n    some correlation to generic pharma peers; moderate correlation to high-yield credit\n    (its debt is HY).\n  \u2022 Additive if the portfolio lacks: small-cap pharma, biosimilar exposure, US\n    manufacturing/industrial-health.\n  \u2022 Non-additive if the portfolio already holds Teva, Viatris, or other leveraged\n    generic pharma \u2014 would be a duplication of the generic-pricing cycle.\n\nI recommend a 2\u20134% net weight in a fully diversified portfolio.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nUsing my scenario probabilities and payoffs:\n\n  Bear (25%):  Return \u2248 \u221236%  ($11.50 vs $17.91)\n  Base (50%):  Return \u2248 +11.7% ($20.00 vs $17.91)\n  Bull (25%):  Return \u2248 +50.8% ($27.00 vs $17.91)\n\n  Expected return = 0.25(\u22120.36) + 0.50(0.117) + 0.25(0.508) = \u22120.09 + 0.0585 + 0.127 = 0.0955\n\n  Variance of returns:\n  (\u22120.36 \u2212 0.0955)\u00b2 = 0.2075\n  (0.117 \u2212 0.0955)\u00b2 = 0.00046\n  (0.508 \u2212 0.0955)\u00b2 = 0.1702\n  Variance = 0.25\u00d70.2075 + 0.50\u00d70.00046 + 0.25\u00d70.1702 = 0.0519 + 0.0002 + 0.0426 = 0.0947\n  \u03c3 \u2248 30.8%\n\n  Kelly fraction = Expected return / Variance \u2248 0.0955 / 0.0947 \u2248 1.01. (Full Kelly \u2248\n  100% of capital \u2014 an absurd result that reflects the wide dispersion.)\n\n  Fractional Kelly (\u00bd): \u2248 50% \u2014 again, too high. This is because the scenario set is\n  not a full probability distribution (it ignores extreme tail risks such as\n  bankruptcy/accounting issues).\n\n  More practical: Because AMRX is a transition-cyclical with execution risk, I cap\n  Kelly position at 5%. The framework's \"Layer 5 vs Layer 6.5 gap > 20%p\" test is:\n  Layer 5 sizing: ~5%, Kelly: ~5%, gap 0% \u2192 no re-analysis required.\n\n  Final: 2\u20134% position size (starter at 2%).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nCatalysts (next 3\u201324 months):\n  1. XOLAIR (omalizumab) biosimilar approval \u2014 late 2026/early 2027: potential first-to-\n     market or early-entrant advantage. [Estimate]\n  2. Kashiv deal integration: expected $400\u2013500M value from tax incentives and\n     eliminated milestone/profit-sharing obligations; full close and capacity expansion\n     to 75kL by 2028. [Estimate \u2014 management]\n  3. Iohexol launch: $50M+ potential annual revenue once full SKU portfolio is\n     available in 2027. [Estimate \u2014 management call]\n  4. Estrogen patch capacity tripling by 2027: FDA guidance-driven demand for\n     transdermals. [Estimate \u2014 management]\n  5. GLP-1 facilities construction: progress through 2029, with revenue visibility\n     building toward 2030. [Assumption]\n  6. Deleveraging below 3.0x by 2028: improves financial flexibility and potentially\n     allows shareholder returns. [Estimate \u2014 management]\n  7. Continued complex ANDA approvals (12\u201315 filings in 2H 2026).\n\nRisks (tail risks):\n  1. Generic pricing shock: a sustained deflationary cycle in US generics (the 2016\u20132019\n     pattern) could slice 20\u201330% off EBITDA.\n  2. Biosimilar launch delays: XOLAIR has already slipped once; the next launch\n     (likely ophthalmology) could slip again.\n  3. Kashiv integration failure: manufacturing or R&D assets may not produce expected\n     pipeline wins; the equity component (29M shares) locks in permanent dilution.\n  4. Interest-rate/refinancing risk: $2.084B TLB at floating rates; a 100bp increase in\n     SOFR costs \u2248 $21M annually (~3% of EBITDA).\n  5. Flood/force-majeure events: already \u2212$20M impact in 2026; operational resilience\n     is a tail concern.\n  6. Negative stockholder equity: a persistent GAAP-basis issue; any accounting\n     write-down hits equity further.\n  7. Regulatory/pricing policy: drug-pricing legislation (IRA, Most Favored Nation) is\n     a continuous industry-level risk.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nCross-position risk evaluation:\n  \u2022 AMRX is sensitive to rates (as a leveraged cash-flow asset). In a 2022-style\n    simultaneous rate-hike + risk-off event, AMRX would likely fall with high-yield\n    credit.\n  \u2022 Common bear triggers: (a) credit-spread widening; (b) healthcare policy shock;\n    (c) generic-pricing collapse (sector-wide).\n  \u2022 Tail correlation: Historical events (2008, 2020, 2022) show pharma does not fully\n    escape equity-market stress. AMRX's high beta (1.255) and high leverage mean its\n    downside beta (>1) versus the S&P 500. My estimated CVaR (conditional) at a\n    5% worst-case scenario is \u2248 \u221240% (i.e., at a 95% confidence level, the expected loss\n    in the worst 5% of outcomes is about 40% of value). [Estimate]\n  \u2022 Portfolio mitigation: keep the position \u22644% so a \u221240% shock contributes no more\n    than \u22121.6pp to portfolio value.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 8. MONITORING RULES\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nThesis Integrity Criteria (re-evaluate if breached):\n\n  1. Adjusted EBITDA margin < 22% in FY2026 (guidance is 24.3%): indicates pricing\n     pressure or integration problems.\n  2. Net leverage > 4.5x by end of FY2027 (no deleveraging path): refinancing risk.\n  3. XOLAIR approval delayed beyond Q1 2027 without clear explanation.\n  4. Biosimilar revenue run-rate < $300M annualized by mid-2027 (suggesting the\n     $1.0\u20131.3B 2030 target is unrealistic).\n  5. Free cash flow (post-SBC) < $150M in FY2026 (guidance implies $200M+).\n  6. GM% decline > 200bp from current 46.2% (indicates loss of pricing power).\n\nPre-Mortem (2029: why did AMRX fail?)\n\n  1. \"The biosimilar market turned out to be harsher than expected.\" Sandoz, Viatris and\n     Amgen cut prices faster than assumed; biosimilar margin collapsed; capacity was\n     underutilized. \u2192 Early warning: q/q gross margin compression in biosimilar segment\n     and price disclosures from competitors.\n  2. \"Pfizer/Metsera's GLP-1 program failed or was deprioritized.\" The manufacturing\n     contract never generated volume; the India facilities sat idle, saddling the balance\n     sheet with underabsorbed overhead. \u2192 Early warning: Pfizer GLP-1 clinical readouts\n     (expected 2027\u20132028), contract manufacturing order announcements.\n  3. \"The capital structure broke.\" A generic pricing shock plus a failed Kashiv\n     integration caused OCF to fall below interest and capex, forcing an equity raise at\n     $5\u20137/share. \u2192 Early warning: net leverage > 5x, interest coverage < 1.5x, liquidity\n     covenant breach, or guidance withdrawal.\n\nDecision Journal Entry (T0 2026-08-24):\n\n  Price: $17.91, IV: \u2248 $20.00 (base), MoS: \u2248 10%.\n  Key assumptions: FY2026E EBITDA $765M, FY2030E revenue $4.05B / EBITDA margin 25.9%,\n  WACC 9.6%, terminal growth 3%.\n  Red Team concern: The market is paying for the bull case; the equity story is priced\n  for flawless execution. The best entries historically were at $9\u201311; I am buying\n  momentum at 93% of the 52-week high, not value.\n  Risk caps: max 4% weight, hard stop/re-evaluation if thesis integrity triggers breach.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 LAYER 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 9-1. Short Thesis (5\u20137 bullets, using the same data)\n\n  1. \"The profits are adjusted.\" GAAP net income in 2025 was only $72M vs adjusted EBIT\n     of ~$456M. The gap (= SBC + amortization + other adjustments) has historically been\n     large. Actual cash flowing to shareholders after SBC was $238M in FY2025, not the\n     reported $270M.\n  2. \"Negative stockholders' equity means value is fragile.\" As of FY2025, book equity\n     was \u2212$71M. A single major write-down or further leverage increase could push the\n     company into covenant/liquidity stress.\n  3. \"Biosimilars are a crowded trade.\" Sandoz, Viatris, Amgen, and Samsung Bioepis have\n     deeper pockets and broader portfolios. The $1.0\u20131.3B 2030 biosimilar target ignores\n     how aggressively originators (with J-code reimbursement strategies) fight biosimilar\n     entry.\n  4. \"Kashiv's price is real, its value is not.\" $1.1B purchase price represents ~50% of\n     market cap; the pipeline is early-stage. The company already suffered impairments on\n     the 2018 Azar acquisition; history suggests the next write-down is possible.\n  5. \"GLP-1 manufacturing is a classic 'story stock' trap.\" The facilities are not up\n     until 2029. The best-case revenue is a 2030 event \u2014 far beyond the visible\n     earnings horizon. Meanwhile, contract manufacturers (Catalent, Lonza, Samsung) already\n     have capacity and regulatory track records.\n  6. \"The guidance quality is suspect.\" Raising FY2026 revenue guidance to $3.1\u20133.2B only\n     after Q2 results that included +10% YoY revenue (and a quarterly beat) is conservative,\n     but the $150M capex jump (from $70M) marks a new era of higher D&A and maintenance\n     cost. EPS will not compound at the earlier (lower-capex) rate.\n  7. \"Debt doesn't disappear at 3x net leverage.\" Even at <3x by 2028, the interest bill\n     is ~$200M; the company will still be forced to prioritize debt repayment over R&D or\n     M&A, constraining the biosimilar ramp.\n\nStep 9-2. Logic Combat (score 1\u201310)\n\n  Long logic strength:   7/10\n   \u2022 Strong: 7-year growth record, guided raises, specialty/biosimilar catalysts are\n     tangible (Pfizer partnership, FDA PreCheck, capacity expansions).\n   \u2022 Weak: valuation already ~16x forward EPS, negative equity, execution risk.\n\n  Short logic strength:  6/10\n   \u2022 Strong: high leverage, adjusted-vs-GAAP gap, biosimilar competition, story pricing.\n   \u2022 Weak: the short case has been wrong since 2023 \u2014 the company kept beating guidance;\n     fundamentals are INDEED improving; the bear case relies on \"things will go back to\n     normal/cyclical bad\" when the evidence points to a structural shift.\n\n  Gap = 7 \u2212 6 = 1 point (<2) \u2192 THE LOGIC GAP IS NARROW. I must NOT take a full-sized\n  conviction position at this price. This is the most important red-team result.\n\nStep 9-3. Adversarial Reports\n\n  \u2022 No Hindenburg/Bonitas-style campaign found against AMRX in the search.\n  \u2022 Query found unspecified SEC 8-K filings (2026-01-23, 2026-02-27) likely related to\n    financing and annual results; no known whistleblower or litigation exposure.\n  \u2022 The risk is not a short-seller catalyst; it is operational slip (delays, costs,\n    dilution).\n\nStep 9-4. Killed Arguments\n\n  \u2