28 for GLP-1/peptide/Kashiv integration].\n- \u0394WC [Assumption]: 15% of incremental revenue (receivables + inventory), consistent with launch-driven pharma.\n- SBC: growing from FY25 $32M [Filed] with revenue at ~1.1% of sales [Assumption].\n- Shares: 319.33M [Actual \u2014 vendor] + 22M [Assumption \u2014 $375M Kashiv equity at ~$17/share] = **342M**.\n\n**Discount-rate derivation:**\n- Risk-free: 4.72% (10Y, 8/21/26) [Fed H.15 \u2014 Actual]\n- Equity risk premium: 4.75% [Assumption \u2014 VIX 15.6, S&P at ~20-21x, moderate conditions]\n- Beta: 1.10 [Assumption \u2014 vendor 1.255 is aggressive; AMRX is defensive-pharma with leverage offset; fundamental beta for specialty/generic pharma 1.0-1.2]\n- Cost of equity: 4.72% + 1.10 \u00d7 4.75% = **9.95% \u2248 10.0%**\n- Pre-tax cost of debt: 6.75% [Estimate \u2014 repriced TLB at SOFR ~3.75% + 3.00% margin \u2248 6.75%; $600M notes ~7.0%]\n- Weights: E = 5.719 / (5.719 + 2.348) = 70.9%; D = 29.1% (net debt $2.348B \u2014 see Section 3 below)\n- **WACC = 0.709 \u00d7 10.0% + 0.291 \u00d7 6.75% \u00d7 (1 \u2212 0.21) = 8.6%**\n- Sensitivity: 50% CI on WACC: 8.0-9.3%; 80% CI: 7.6-10.0% [Assumption]\n\n**Net debt construction (disclosed, per pack rules):** LT debt noncurrent $2.565B + current portion $0.007B + finance leases $0.058B = $2.630B; less cash $0.282B \u2192 **$2.348B**. Operating leases excluded from base case (add $59M if included \u2014 immaterial). Vendor total-debt cross-check $2.689B [Aggregator] \u2014 unresolved discrepancy of $59M, treated in Bear/Distress scenarios.\n\n**PV calculation (mid-year convention, WACC 8.6%):**\nPV(FCFF 2026-2035) = $4.750B\nTerminal EV = 8.5x \u00d7 $1.364B = $11.594B; PV(TV) = $5.296B\nEnterprise value = $10.046B\nLess net debt $2.348B \u2192 Equity value = $7.698B\n\u00f7 342M shares \u2192 **Intrinsic Value (Base) = $22.50/share**\n\nMultiple cross-checks:\n- EV/EBITDA: 2026E EV/EBITDA = 8.067/0.765 = 10.5x (current) vs 8.5x terminal \u2192 market is paying a premium to the exit.\n- P/E: 2026E adjusted EPS $1.01 [Company guidance \u2014 Actual] \u2192 current P/E 17.7x; fair-value P/E at IV 22.50 = 22.3x.\n- 2030E EPS cross-check: 2030 NI \u2248 $520M [Model estimate] / 342M = $1.52; at 16x = $24.3; discounted at 10% for 4 years = $16.6 \u2192 current price is *approximately* where the 2030 plan at 16x lands today. My DCF's 8.5x EBITDA exit \u2248 12-13x P/E at 2035 (equivalent). This is the honest tension: **the DCF's margin/FCFF path must beat the multiple verdict by ~25% to justify the model; that is the crux of the thesis.**\n\n**Engine 4 \u2014 Reverse DCF (current price $17.91):**\nImplied EV = $8.067B; subtract PV of base-case FCFF 2026-2030 \u2248 $2.48B (at 9%) \u2192 implied 2030 terminal PV today $5.59B; grossed up at 9% \u2192 $8.60B EV in 2030. At 9x EV/EBITDA, implied 2030 EBITDA = **$956M** \u2014 i.e., the market prices ~$3.8-4.0B revenue at ~24-25% margin, or roughly 15-20% below my base case. **The market is NOT paying for the full company 2030 target; it is paying for execution of the near-term plan with a haircut.** No excessive optimism embedded; optionality is free-ish.\n\n**Tornado sensitivity (\u00b11\u03c3, Base IV $22.50):**\n1. 2030 revenue \u00b1$400M ($4.0-4.8B): IV \u2248 $19.5-25.5\n2. 2030 EBITDA margin \u00b1200bps (24.3-28.3%): IV \u2248 $19.8-26.0\n3. WACC \u00b170bps (7.9-9.3%): IV \u2248 $26.9-20.0\n4. Terminal EV/EBITDA \u00b11.5x (7-10x): IV \u2248 $19.8-25.6\n5. Kashiv added debt/milestones \u00b1$200M: IV \u00b1$0.6\n**Top-3 decision variables (Thesis KPIs): 2030 revenue, 2030 EBITDA margin, WACC.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 3.5-1 \u2014 Competitive Response (\u22122 to +2):** If Amneal launches 3-4 biosimilars/yr, incumbents (Samsung Bioepis, Sandoz, Celltrion for biosims; Novartis/Amgen for branded defenders) respond with aggressive contracting, co-pay programs, and device iteration. Expected margin erosion on mature biosimilars in years 3-5: \u221225-40% [Estimate from industry base rates]. Net second-order impact: **\u22122** (bearish margin path post-2030, partially captured in the fade to 25%).\n\n**Step 3.5-2 \u2014 Reflexivity:** Positive price loop exists (equity currency for Kashiv; talent; customer trust); negative loop absent at $18 (no distress signal). If price revisits $9-11, the negative reflexivity (supplier/customer confidence) would be material.\n\n**Step 3.5-3 \u2014 Customer Behavior Evolution:** CREXONT moving from early-adopter (movement-disorder specialists) to mainstream (80% of new scripts from general neurologists) [Actual \u2014 Q2-26 call]. Biosimilar adoption accelerates post-LOE; payer mandates and interchangeability drive volume. LTV/CAC: biosimilar LTV high once formulary-listed, but price erosion is secular; specialty LTV growing with CREXONT positioning toward first-line.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (Bayesian)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 4-1/2 \u2014 Scenario Composition (probabilities sum to 100%)**\n| Scenario | Prob | 2030 Revenue | 2030 EBITDA% | IV/share | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bull | 20% | $4.6B | 27.0% | $33 | All launches hit (Xolair early-27, lanreotide, 12+ biosims), GLP-1 contract scales, CREXONT first-line, Kashiv synergies $500M |\n| Base | 55% | $4.4B | 26.3% | $22.50 | Company plan at midpoint; some delays; competitive erosion normal |\n| Bear | 20% | $3.8B | 22.0% | $13 | Biosimilar price war, Xolair slips >1yr, GLP-1 volumes disappoint, Kashiv integration problems, leverage ~4x |\n| Distress | 5% | $3.4B | 18.0% | $6 | Covenant breach, dilutive equity raise, flood/supply-chain crisis, strategic review/restructuring |\n\n**Step 4-3 \u2014 Bayesian Update:** Prior (platform-transition base rate): 25%. Updates: +10pp (FY26 raised guidance \u00d73; Q1/Q2-26 beat [Actual]), +5pp (lanreotide Q3-26; Xolair under review [Actual]), \u22123pp (Xolair delay from mid-26 to late-26/early-27; flood \u2212$20M; AvKARE \u22124% [Actual]). Posterior for combined base+bull success: **~75%** (consistent with scenario probabilities). Base-case probability (55%) is the single highest \u2014 satisfied.\n\n**Step 4-4/5 \u2014 Expected Price & Margin of Safety:**\nEV = 0.20(33) + 0.55(22.50) + 0.20(13) + 0.05(6) = **$22.20**\n50% CI: $18.50-$27.00; 80% CI: $13.50-$33.00 [Estimate]\nMoS = ($22.20 \u2212 $17.91)/$22.20 = **19.3% \u2014 Adequate** (15-30%).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5 \u2014 TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n**Step 4.5-1/2 \u2014 Market-Implied Horizon vs Mine:**\nMarket horizon \u2248 2 years (it prices 2026-27 guidance and a modest discount to the 2030 plan, implied by the reverse DCF). My horizon: 5 years (the full 2026-2030 target period). Spread = **~3 years** \u2192 a genuine arbitrage exists *if* the plan executes. At 5 years, 2030E EPS $1.52 \u00d7 16x = $24.30; at a 10% equity discount rate that is $15.10 present \u2014 so the market today is paying approximately fair value for 2030 at 16x. The arbitrage therefore comes **not from cheap entry at $17.91** but from: (a) the probability that the market will re-rate to 18-20x if execution is flawless; (b) upside to my $22.50 DCF if cash flows beat the multiple-implied path. Conclusion: modest, opportunity exists but requires patience; it is NOT a \"2